LOGBOOK.md:10:- RUNTIME GATE: The no-download audit scope is now an exact non-empty contract over `App/`, `Sources/`, and `relay/` plus all relevant Swift/Go/Objective-C/C/C++ source kinds. It explicitly excludes Go test tooling and permits only syntactically explicit local-file `Data(contentsOf:)` reads; Foundation network loaders, ambiguous URL reads, process/shell execution, Go HTTP/process/plugin surfaces, HTTP locators, symlinked scope entries, missing roots, and partial/disabled scan policy fail closed.
LOGBOOK.md:11:- VERIFICATION: Sixteen focused Python tests reproduce the reviewer findings and adjacent branch/tag/latest/query/fragment/placeholder/host, license-remapping, component-cross-record, runtime-scope, and symlink bypasses. Supply-chain/asset audits, 26 asset-manifest Python tests, 4 Swift manifest tests, all relay Go packages, 35 relay-release tests, Go vet, unsigned Swift build, Black, Swift format, Actionlint, JSON parsing, privacy scan, diff check, and byte-identical double generation all exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:17:- GATE: `make relay-supply-chain-audit` fails on lock/source/recipe/toolchain/license/notice/inventory/provenance/manifest drift, sensitive or workstation data, mutable dependency URLs, and application runtime code-download surfaces. Deterministic generation is byte-idempotent, the focused Python/Swift asset suites pass, and the pinned relay Go tests plus vet remain green.
LOGBOOK.md:28:- ARCHITECTURE: The Release provider is universal `arm64`/`x86_64`, embeds `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter` plus HEV/libssh2 production symbols, contains no `CReluxNativeFixture` or forbidden runtime-loader symbol, links only system frameworks/libraries, and embeds a checksum-valid four-platform relay payload. No signing overlay, embedded private-key payload, signing, installation, launch, VPN preference/tunnel, route, or DNS mutation was used.
LOGBOOK.md:52:- BUILD GRAPH: The minimal provider intentionally depends on `ReluxTunnelCore`, not `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter`. Adding the adapter exposed an existing SwiftPM/Xcode transitive binary-output collision: both `ReluxLibSSH2` and `CReluxNativeFixture` produce `include/module.modulemap`. Packet and transport implementation is out of this task's scope; the later runtime-integration task owns resolving that collision before adding the adapter dependency.
LOGBOOK.md:84:- COVERAGE: Host-before-auth rejection variants, approved algorithms and opaque signing, direct/exec/upload isolation, bounded EAGAIN/backpressure, byte/time/manual/server rekey traffic, keepalive scheduling/failure, 15 cleanup scopes, complete available metrics, privacy-safe errors, resource baselines, and every deferred runtime surface are asserted. Exact M3 ownership remains `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
LOGBOOK.md:152:- DEFERRED GAPS: Receive-window consumer credit/cap, RFC channel-open reasons, exact exec-exit/core-dump metadata, and deep server-rekey/keepalive observability remain explicit `unsupported` or runtime `notReported`, owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`. Physical scale, memory, and soak remain separate M3 rows; no exact value was inferred from the C fixture's 16 observed server-KEX transitions.
LOGBOOK.md:158:- IMPLEMENTATION: The macOS profile-driven SSH boundary now projects typed profile, path, connect, negotiation, host-policy, Keychain, public-key authentication, cancellation/user-stop, and session-close causes into the fixed `sshProfileBootstrap` provider domain. Public errors and runtime snapshots carry only stable code/stage, configuration generation, user-action category, retry disposition, and reviewed algorithm/endpoint-family context; arbitrary underlying prose and identifying values are structurally unrepresentable.
LOGBOOK.md:159:- RETRY POLICY: Corrupt or unsupported profiles, host mismatch/change/revocation/unsupported algorithms, inaccessible or malformed credentials, and authentication rejection are terminal until configuration, trust, or credential state changes. Only bounded timeouts, unavailable paths, and an explicit allowlist of transient transport interruptions are marked `retryableLater`; this task records classification and adds no reconnect behavior.
LOGBOOK.md:161:- VERIFICATION: Golden taxonomy, hostile-text/prohibited-data, cancellation, NSError redaction, socket/URL, NWError, OSStatus, runtime-snapshot, selected libssh2 timeout, and live authentication-timeout tests pass. Recursive Swift format lint, boundary/native/libssh2 verification, all 425 Swift tests in 35 suites, `swift build`, and `git diff --check` exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:181:- GENERATION SAFETY: A fresh `SSHProfileRuntimeSnapshotLoader` owns one runtime start. A present exact five-field v1 start request binds `configurationGeneration` to the SHA-256 digest of the exact stored canonical snapshot bytes before first capture; nil options may capture the validated stored value. Byte-identical rereads return the original capture, while stale requests, same-generation field replacement, newer-generation replacement, and concurrent competing captures fail with the fixed privacy-safe `profileGenerationMismatch` error.
LOGBOOK.md:183:- VERIFICATION: Thirteen focused loader tests, 52 combined loader/runtime-start/session tests, and the complete 392-test/32-suite run pass, including HEV. `swift build`, recursive format lint, core-boundary validation, and `git diff --check` all exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:211:- PORTAL RESULT: Xcode automatic provisioning created or updated exactly the four r12 macOS identifiers and downloaded one device-bound Mac Team Provisioning Profile for each: `works.relux.tunnel.mac`, `.mac.tunnel`, `.probe.mac`, and `.probe.mac.tunnel`. Network Extensions is profile-authorized on hosts and providers alike; no portal reauthentication or two-factor challenge occurred.
LOGBOOK.md:214:- ANOMALY: The portal/profile phase succeeded, but final local codesigning returned `errSecInternalComponent` for each harness target because unattended signing-key access was unavailable. A separate all-target no-sign compile succeeded. Read-only Safari automation also remained unavailable (timeout exit 142), consistent with APC34W-B5; neither issue caused a portal reauthentication challenge or invalidated the downloaded profiles.
LOGBOOK.md:372:- DECISION: owner is **`TASK-260715-1uxx3i`** — an **existing** task. Its `macos-15` minimum lane already exists in the deployment-target policy and reading the runner's own `application.sb` is a credential-free static read inside its AC2; it already reaches this contract transitively via `TASK-260715-uyju7n`, so **no board element was created** and no edge added. `TASK-260715-1o9wjz` consumes the same answer at runtime. Tracked as **OC-5**.
LOGBOOK.md:496:- DECISION: `ReluxProxyMac`'s sandbox is recorded as a **project** decision — `.spec/platform-distribution.md` embeds Sparkle only in the sandboxed hardened-runtime host with `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach exceptions, per ADR-018 — not an Apple rule. The contract now carries `requirementSource` per row (`apple-requirement` / `project-architecture` / `sandbox-consequence` / `platform-inherent`) and validator R21 rejects a host row claiming an Apple requirement.
LOGBOOK.md:515:- NOTE: The trap that made this invisible is that the entitlement is *accepted* everywhere — portal, profile, and `codesign` all succeed. Only a runtime `SecItem` call in the provider fails, which is M1-deep. Least privilege caught it because "grant only what the target can actually use" forced the question.
LOGBOOK.md:520:- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
LOGBOOK.md:530:- ROOT CAUSE: `TunnelProviderAdapter.providerDidFail` was `nonisolated` and deferred *all* admission into a freshly spawned unstructured `Task`. Swift orders nothing between unstructured tasks contending for the same actor, so two sequential provider-failure callbacks could enter `handleProviderFailure` in reverse order. The `cancelTunnelIssuedGeneration` ledger enforced exactly-once but had no notion of *which* call arrived first, so the second error (`internalInvariant` 1009) beat the first (`runtimeStartupFailed` 1007) in **10 of 40** focused runs (~25%).
LOGBOOK.md:599:- SCHEMA: Manifest v1 records all sixteen fixed-unit per-metric comparison results, paired run/sample IDs, signed integer point effects and absolute/PPM bootstrap bounds, exact rational coverage and integer interval ranks, aggregate/per-metric safety status, accepted-update lineage, and simultaneous median/p95/p99 latency triplets. Production authorization requires concrete reviewer/time, available classifications, stable group, candidate count, exact coverage, nonempty lineage, and all prior safety/privacy/authority gates.
LOGBOOK.md:606:- SAFETY: Schema v1 accepts a production-authorized accepted row whose reviewer, review time, classification, multiplicity group, and candidate count are all explicitly unavailable; it also accepts unavailable/invalid-environment rows with `reasonCode=measured-pass`. These schema-expressible authority/status contradictions require analysis rework before the validator can implement v1 without inventing policy.
LOGBOOK.md:611:- REPRODUCIBILITY: The protocol now fixes the RFC 8785 canonical row projection, identifier/seed relationships, SplitMix64 stream derivation, packet order and loss/jitter sampling, exact paired-effect integer rounding, 10,000-resample bootstrap index mapping, multiplicity group/coverage, quantiles, and inclusive equality/strict regression-boundary classification.
LOGBOOK.md:624:- DECISION: Pin official Sparkle 2.9.4 exactly in the macOS host; require pre-extraction EdDSA verification plus a signed feed with failure expiration disabled; publish only final Developer ID-signed, hardened-runtime, notarized/stapled DMGs from a public read-only HTTPS origin after asset-first verification. Stable is Sparkle's default channel and opt-in prerelease uses `prerelease`; private GitHub remains manual distribution.
LOGBOOK.md:633:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
LOGBOOK.md:642:- SECURITY: Identity is one canonical JSON line capped at 512 bytes. It validates the injected semantic version and lowercase 40-hex commit, hashes the running executable through `/proc/self/exe` on Linux or the cgo-free standard executable path on Darwin, and keeps all diagnostics on stderr. Protocol stdout has one writer and contains only hello/framed bytes; the production dependency graph includes neither `net` nor `os/exec` and creates no runtime files.
LOGBOOK.md:647:- DECISION: `relay/toolchain-manifest-v1.json` is the checked-in authority for Go 1.26.5 `gc`/internal-linker identity, official Go and Syft host archive hashes, the standard-library-only module lock, license hashes/identifiers, four target/CPU baselines, the absence of build SDKs/sysroots, macOS 12.0, deterministic flags, and the exact actions/checkout v7.0.1 revision. Linux static linkage does not justify a kernel-version floor; the declared runtime baseline is native unprivileged Ubuntu 24.04 on both x86_64 and arm64 CI fixtures.
LOGBOOK.md:648:- SECURITY: Target builds construct an environment allowlist and isolate `HOME`, `TMPDIR`, `GOCACHE`, `GOMODCACHE`, and `GOPATH` per target below `.build/relay/work`; `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, `GOPROXY=off`, `GOSUMDB=off`, `GOVCS=off`, `GOENV=off`, and `GOWORK=off` prevent undeclared downloads or workstation credential/config reuse.
LOGBOOK.md:650:- REWORK: Provenance now compares every installed Go directory/file, mode, and content digest to the checksum-pinned archive and rejects missing, added, changed, duplicate, traversing, symlink, hardlink, device, or other unsafe entries. Exact negative fixtures cover runtime and standard-library tampering, missing Go, missing `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, checkout pin drift, and `GOAMD64=v1`/`GOARM64=v8.0` metadata drift.
